Exercise the interrupted-worker path. In Main declare main throws Exception. Start the Swing event queue with an empty invokeAndWait call. Create a disabled button and status label, start and then interrupt a ReadyTask worker, join it, then flush the Swing event queue before reading the actual status and button state.

Before the existing headless check in `Main.java`, add this console check. Keep the original sample message and the window code.

SwingUtilities.invokeAndWait(() -> {});
    JLabel status = new JLabel("Not checked");
    JButton button = new JButton("Check ready");
    button.setEnabled(false);
    Thread worker = new Thread(new ReadyTask(status, button));
    worker.start();
    worker.interrupt();
    worker.join();
    SwingUtilities.invokeAndWait(() -> {
      System.out.println(status.getText());
      System.out.println("Button enabled: " + button.isEnabled());
    });

